[Music]  an average of 30 container ships has sat  anchored daily  just off the coast of los angeles this  year the normal number of  container ships at anchor is zero to one  this ship the everfront not to be  confused with the now famous ever given  was waiting for 13 days seeing vessels  at anchorage outside the nation's  largest container port  does not happen every day and it is a  once in about a decade  situation that we see everfront was part  of a bottleneck in the supply chain  that's still straining the neighboring  ports  which handle about a third of all u.s  imports i don't think it's an  exaggeration to say that the ports have  never really seen  this confluence of stresses that they've  had to deal with  so what's causing this offshore cue how  do these waiting ships impact customers  onshore  and when will it return to normal i  think we got here because  we all had to stay home experts say if  there's one cause for the backup  it's covet 19. staying home leads you to  go  all right my desk isn't great if i've  got one my chair isn't comfortable even  if i've got one  i might need a new computer monitor i  might need  a webcam actually i can't go to the gym  so i'm going to go buy  a peloton or a running machine or some  dumbbells  for many americans that has meant  placing online orders for those items  most of which aren't made in the u.s  seaborn imports to the u.s  started surging in the late summer as  manufacturers and retailers tried to  rebuild inventories that were depleted  by shoppers in the early months of the  pandemic  we had 57 extra ships arrive in the fall  above normal levels which choked the  system  because at that same time the goods  movement system assure  the terminals the trains the trucks the  warehouses the forklift drivers  had some fraction of the workforce out  with kovid  and a different fraction out because of  contact tracing  so just when there's more cargo there's  a less efficient  goods movement the system ashore so the  ships backed up  but shipments continued peaking in  february when 177 container ships  carrying more than 800  000 containers arrived at the southern  california ports  that's 31 percent more ships and 49  more containers than the same month last  year  this gap highlights a related issue the  ships  are getting bigger there's been a  significant increase in the number of  the very biggest boats that come in i'm  also very efficient when they're  at sea they're a devil to unpack and a  devil to park  and so it takes longer to get all of  them sorted out  so the ships continue to wait an average  of seven and a half days before reaching  the port  and that means consumers are waiting too  okay peloton let's go  this is something buyers of peloton  bikes may already know  the company said in its latest earnings  report that backups contributed to  recent shipping delays  i'm sure you've seen that on a lot of  e-commerce sites it's taking us longer  than normal to  deliver to you and so you know there's  that kind of customer disappointment  angle and companies will then need to  think okay how do i avoid that in the  future  time come on let's get this workout  started for peloton the answer has been  to spend more  the company said it would spend 100  million dollars in transportation  including air shipments in the first  half of the year to get around the  bottlenecks of the port mom and pop  retailers also have products they'd hope  to sell  stuck on these ships and many of the  smallest shops  can't afford to fly products in instead  but the ships  aren't the only issue here the entire  supply chain from ports to warehouses to  distribution centers  is inundated with cargo so if the cargo  is not moving  quickly from the ship to the shore out  through the warehouses to the consumer  everything else is going to start to  back up this congestion which was driven  by demand for shipping  also means that the cost of shipping has  gone up  the current global average shipping rate  is nearly four times what it was in  january 2020 according to data from s p  global platz someone's going to pay for  that it's either going to come off  company's profits  or they're going to pass it through to  consumers the longer it lasts  the more likely it is that consumers are  going to pay it  at the ports workers are pushing to get  the anchor chips moving  pre-pandemic we were welcoming on  average  10 container vessels in to work at the  port  loading and unloading containers every  day during this surge  we've been averaging 15 vessels working  every day in the port  that's a 50 percent jump in productivity  alongside our vessels every day  sirocca says the port's short-term  strategy focuses on vaccinating workers  maximizing efficiencies and asking  importers to more quickly pick up cargo  that he predicts will help clear the  backlog of ships by this summer  any longer could bring more problems we  have a short window here before our  traditional peak season kicks off  in august for back to school other  retail requirements and the  all-important year-end holiday  with americans receiving a fresh round  of stimulus this bottleneck could  intensify  and how states and the federal  government proceed with reopening  may impact that if we're going to spend  another summer lockdown then people are  going to be buying  you know new lawn chairs and a whole  bunch of stuff they didn't buy  last year if everything's open again and  people can go on vacation  it's all services spending we're not  going to buy stuff  so the demand for shipping will drop off  relatively quickly

